<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black September</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1970">1970</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Black September was the armed conflict of September 1970 in which the Jordanian army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-of-jordan">King Hussein</a> fought the Palestinian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">Palestine Liberation Organization</a>, which had built a state within a state in Jordan. A Syrian armoured incursion in support of the Palestinians was turned back. By July 1971 the fighters had been expelled, and the PLO moved its base to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-september.jpg" alt="Black September" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smoke_rises_above_Amman_during_Black_September,_1_October_1970.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a> of 1967 Palestinian fedayeen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> relocated to Jordan and stepped up attacks on Israel and the Israeli-occupied West Bank. They were headquartered at the border town of Karameh, which Israel targeted in the Battle of Karameh in 1968, bringing a surge of Arab support. The strength of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">Palestine Liberation Organization</a> (PLO), led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasser-arafat">Yasser Arafat</a>, grew, and by early 1970 leftist groups within it were calling for the overthrow of Jordan's Hashemite monarchy, leading to violent clashes in June 1970.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-of-jordan">King Hussein</a> feared that an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">PLO</a> administration would threaten his kingdom, and he hesitated to expel the fighters. The Dawson's Field hijackings of 6 September 1970 ended his patience. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a> for the Liberation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> seized three airliners; two jets landed at Azraq, and a third plane was brought to Zarqa on 9 September. The planes were blown up before the international press on 12 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The main fighting ran from 16 to 27 September 1970. On 17 September the Jordanian Army surrounded cities with a strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">PLO</a> presence, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a> and Irbid, and attacked fedayeen posts in the refugee camps. The next day 10,000 Syrian troops bearing Palestine Liberation Army markings advanced towards Irbid, which the fedayeen had declared a liberated city. On 22 September the Syrians withdrew after heavy losses to a coordinated Jordanian air and ground offensive.</p><p>Pressure from Arab states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> led Hussein to halt his offensive, and on 13 October he signed an agreement with Arafat to regulate the fedayeen's presence. The army attacked again in January 1971 and drove the fighters from the cities. On 23 July 1971 some 2,000 surrendered after being encircled in the Ajlun offensive, which formally ended the conflict. The fedayeen were allowed to relocate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Wasfi Tal's civilian government purged the bureaucracy and military of supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, and closed many newspapers. Palestinians and Jordanians fought on both sides in 1970, so the conflict did not reflect a Jordanian-Palestinian divide, but it made one possible. Hussein's resilience impressed the West and Israel, and Nixon ordered $10 million in aid to be delivered to Jordan.</p><p>Fatah members founded the Black September Organization in 1971. Its members assassinated Tal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> on 28 November 1971, and in 1972 it carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> massacre of Israeli athletes. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> the enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine-liberation-organization">PLO</a> presence stirred internal unrest and helped to precipitate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanese-civil-war">Lebanese Civil War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black September” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army lost two-thirds of the Golan Heights to Israel in under 48 hours in the Six-Day War of 1967, retreated from Jordan in <b>Black September</b> 1970, and with Egypt launched the Yom Kippur War on 6 October 1973, in which Israel reversed its gains and destroyed Quneitra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Black September</b> militants killed two members of the Israeli team and took nine hostage.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-5">5 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Jordan lost the West Bank to Israel in 1967 and expelled the Palestinian armed groups in the conflict of <b>Black September</b> in 1970.</blockquote><a href="/state/hashemite-kingdom-of-jordan">Hashemite Kingdom of Jordan</a></li></ul></section>
